00:40:31,260 --> 00:40:34,920
And so if you think about the terrain
of the seafloor,

710
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:39,080
those mountains are made up of hard rock
or a hard surface.

711
00:40:39,080 --> 00:40:42,860
And so the areas in between the mountains
are what we call abyssal plain.

712
00:40:42,860 --> 00:40:44,340
And that's often soft sediment.

713
00:40:44,340 --> 00:40:47,400
So that's where all of that
debris has fallen down.

714
00:40:48,060 --> 00:40:52,340
And what we found is that most of these,
like what we call benthic communities

715
00:40:52,340 --> 00:40:56,120
or animals that live
kind of on the surface of rocks,

716
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:59,280
they really like seamounts
because they provide that habitat,

717
00:40:59,280 --> 00:41:01,140
which is a place that they settle

718
00:41:01,140 --> 00:41:04,140
and then they grow
because they're not moving around.

719
00:41:04,400 --> 00:41:07,460
And so what we've,
what the scientific community

720
00:41:07,460 --> 00:41:10,800
has kind of started to understand
is that seamounts provide

721
00:41:10,800 --> 00:41:16,140
these kind of hotspots across the world's
oceans for these communities to exist.

722
00:41:17,840 --> 00:41:18,560
Even if there's

723
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:21,560
not land nearby
or other, other types of input.

724
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:25,400
I've got another question
from someone here at the Museum.

725
00:41:25,940 --> 00:41:29,040
Q: What is your favorite species of coral?

726
00:41:29,720 --> 00:41:32,580
>> Oh, there we go.
>> That's my favourite question.

727
00:41:32,580 --> 00:41:36,120
I wish I could show you a picture.
My favorite species

728
00:41:36,140 --> 00:41:40,260
or group of coral are called bubblegum
corals because they're bright pink

729
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:42,500
and they're very beautiful.
>> Really pretty.

730
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:46,520
But they can live really deep
in, in the ocean as well as

731
00:41:46,520 --> 00:41:47,840
and they're very old, right?

732
00:41:47,840 --> 00:41:48,060
Yeah.

733
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:52,220
They can grow, most of the species that
live in the deep ocean grow very slowly.

734
00:41:52,220 --> 00:41:53,840
And they live to be very old.

735
00:41:53,840 --> 00:41:59,280
So the oldest coral ever age-dated
is over 4000 years old.

736
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:03,600
So, these are quite, quite ancient

737
00:42:04,740 --> 00:42:05,420
animals.

738
00:42:05,420 --> 00:42:07,080
Well, this might be a question for you, Jen.

739
00:42:07,080 --> 00:42:09,120
It's come
all the way from Christmas Island.

740
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:13,500
Q: How long does it take to train
as a taxonomist to do the works

741
00:42:13,500 --> 00:42:17,600
that, the staff at the museum
and scientists are doing?

742
00:42:19,560 --> 00:42:21,440
So, most of our curators,

743
00:42:21,440 --> 00:42:25,200
have PhDs,
so they usually do an undergraduate degree

744
00:42:25,200 --> 00:42:29,480
in marine science, and then they'll go on
and either doing an honours or a master's.

745
00:42:29,480 --> 00:42:32,480
So that's sort of anywhere
from 4 to 5 years.

746
00:42:32,820 --> 00:42:33,540
And then they'll go on

747
00:42:33,540 --> 00:42:37,080
and do a PhD, which usually takes anywhere
from 3 to 4 years.

748
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:38,460
And then they might do what

749
00:42:38,460 --> 00:42:42,080
we call a postdoctoral fellowship,
which is another year or two after that.

750
00:42:42,500 --> 00:42:45,840
So yeah, a fair chunk of time,

751
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:49,580
I would say, anywhere
from sort of 7 to 9 years.

752
00:42:50,760 --> 00:42:51,540
Yeah. Wow.

753
00:42:51,540 --> 00:42:54,080
And we've got a question here
from the kids at the front.

754
00:42:54,080 --> 00:42:57,960
Q: How long has OceanX been
an exploring company?

755
00:42:59,040 --> 00:43:00,360
Yeah, that's a good question.

756
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:03,980
So the ship started exploring in 2021.

757
00:43:03,980 --> 00:43:07,920
So we have been kind of
continuously exploring different parts

758
00:43:07,920 --> 00:43:11,660
of the world's oceans,
for the last five years or so.

759
00:43:13,620 --> 00:43:14,180
I think we have time

760
00:43:14,180 --> 00:43:17,180
for maybe one more question.

761
00:43:18,080 --> 00:43:22,740
Q: How many ammonite fossils
have you found starting from?

762
00:43:22,740 --> 00:43:25,740
today?

763
00:43:27,080 --> 00:43:29,640
We haven't,
so we haven't found any ammonite fossils.

764
00:43:29,640 --> 00:43:34,920
But on a previous expedition we did see
when we were around the waters of Palau,

765
00:43:34,940 --> 00:43:38,040
which is in the Pacific,
we saw live Nautilus,

766
00:43:38,040 --> 00:43:40,760
which are essentially
the descendants of ammonites.

767
00:43:40,760 --> 00:43:42,200
Yeah,

768
00:43:42,200 --> 00:43:42,960
yeah. Wow.

769
00:43:42,960 --> 00:43:46,020
Okay, folks, sadly,
that's all we have time for today.

